INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, featured_image, image_alt, category, tags, author, meta_title, meta_description, status, published_at, created_at) VALUES ('commercial-fridge-running-warm-checklist', 'Commercial Fridge Running Warm? A Checklist for West London Food Businesses', 'Display fridge, dairy cabinet or cold room creeping above temperature? What to do first, the checks staff can safely make, and when to call an engineer.', '<p>When a commercial fridge starts running warm, the clock is ticking on your stock, your food safety records and — if it&#39;s a customer-facing display — your sales. Whether it&#39;s a cold room in a restaurant kitchen, a dairy cabinet in a convenience store or a back-bar cooler in a pub, the steps below will help you protect your stock, rule out the simple causes and give an engineer the information they need.</p>
<h2>The temperatures that matter</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chilled food:</strong> in England, the law requires chilled food to be kept at <strong>8°C or below</strong>. Most businesses aim for <strong>5°C or lower</strong> to give themselves a safety margin.</li>
<li><strong>Frozen food:</strong> freezers and freezer rooms typically run at <strong>-18°C or colder</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your own food safety management system (for many small businesses that&#39;s the FSA&#39;s <em>Safer Food, Better Business</em> pack) should set out your target temperatures and what to do when something goes wrong. Follow it.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Act straight away</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Record the temperature and the time.</strong> Check the unit&#39;s display and, if you can, use a probe thermometer to check the temperature of the food itself — air temperature rises and falls faster than product temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Keep doors closed.</strong> Every opening lets more warm air in.</li>
<li><strong>Move high-risk stock</strong> — cooked meats, dairy, fish, prepared foods — to working refrigeration if you have it.</li>
<li><strong>Decide what to do with stock that&#39;s been too warm</strong> in line with your food safety procedures. If you&#39;re unsure, don&#39;t sell it.</li>
<li><strong>Write down what you did.</strong> A clear record protects you if an environmental health officer asks later.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Step 2: Quick checks staff can make</h2>
<p>These checks are safe for staff to do and solve a surprising number of &quot;breakdowns&quot;.</p>
<h3>Power and controller</h3>
<ul>
<li>Is the unit switched on and the display lit?</li>
<li>Has the <strong>set point been changed</strong> — accidentally or by someone trying to &quot;turn it up&quot;?</li>
<li>Is there an <strong>alarm or error code</strong> on the controller? Note it down or take a photo.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Doors and seals</h3>
<ul>
<li>Has a door been <strong>propped open</strong> or left ajar during a delivery or a busy service?</li>
<li>Are door <strong>gaskets split, flattened or hanging loose</strong>? Do doors close and seal by themselves?</li>
<li>On freezer rooms, is there <strong>ice around the door frame</strong>? That can mean a failed frame heater.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Loading and airflow</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Open multideck cabinets</strong> rely on a curtain of cold air down the front. Stock stacked past the <strong>load line</strong>, or packaging hanging over shelf edges, breaks that curtain.</li>
<li><strong>Cold rooms and reach-in fridges</strong> need space for air to circulate. Boxes stacked tight against the evaporator (the fan unit) choke the airflow.</li>
<li>Have you just loaded a large delivery or <strong>warm product</strong>? A unit can take hours to pull back down.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Heat around the unit</h3>
<ul>
<li>Is the <strong>condenser</strong> — the coil and fan that get rid of heat, usually behind a grille at the bottom or on an outdoor unit — clogged with dust or kitchen grease?</li>
<li>Has something been put in front of the vents, or is the unit now next to an oven, a glasswasher or in direct sun?</li>
<li>For cold rooms and cellar cooling, is the <strong>outdoor condensing unit</strong> clear of leaves, rubbish and stacked crates?</li>
</ul>
<h3>Ice and water</h3>
<ul>
<li>Is there <strong>heavy ice</strong> on the evaporator coil? That usually points to a defrost problem or warm, humid air getting in.</li>
<li>Is water <strong>pooling inside or leaking</strong> onto the floor? A blocked drain line is common — and a slip hazard. Put a sign out and mop up.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: When to call an engineer</h2>
<p>Call for a repair if, after those checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>The temperature keeps rising, or won&#39;t come back down within a reasonable time.</li>
<li>The compressor isn&#39;t running, or is starting and stopping every few minutes.</li>
<li>Fans aren&#39;t turning, or are noisy.</li>
<li>The evaporator keeps icing up.</li>
<li>You notice <strong>oily marks</strong> on pipework (a possible sign of a refrigerant leak).</li>
<li>The controller shows an alarm you can&#39;t clear.</li>
</ul>
<p>Handling refrigerant gases legally requires certified engineers, so leave anything involving the sealed system to a professional.</p>
<p>To help the engineer, have ready: the <strong>make and model</strong> (on the rating plate inside the unit or on the back), the <strong>temperatures you&#39;ve recorded</strong>, any <strong>error codes</strong>, and when the problem started.</p>
<p>We repair <a href="/services/cold-room-repair/">cold rooms and walk-in freezers</a>, <a href="/services/display-fridge-repair/">display fridges and serve-over counters</a>, <a href="/services/dairy-cabinet-repair/">multideck dairy cabinets</a>, <a href="/services/bar-cooler-repair/">bar coolers</a>, <a href="/services/cellar-cooler-repair/">cellar cooling</a> and <a href="/services/ice-machine-repair/">ice machines</a> for businesses across Southall, Hounslow, Hayes, Heathrow and the rest of West London. <a href="/book/?service=cold-room-repair">Call us or book online</a> and tell us what you&#39;re seeing.</p>
<h2>Preventing the next breakdown</h2>
<p>Most commercial refrigeration failures build up slowly. A few habits make a real difference:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Log temperatures at least twice a day</strong> — you&#39;ll spot a unit that&#39;s struggling before it fails.</li>
<li><strong>Clean condenser coils regularly</strong>, more often in kitchens where grease builds up quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Respect load lines</strong> and keep space around evaporators.</li>
<li><strong>Check door gaskets</strong> weekly and replace damaged ones promptly — they&#39;re cheap compared with lost stock and energy.</li>
<li><strong>Keep outdoor units clear</strong>, especially before summer.</li>
<li>Consider <strong>planned maintenance</strong>: periodic professional checks of coils, fans, defrost operation, controllers and electrical connections catch problems early.</li>
</ul>
<p>A warm fridge is stressful in the middle of service. Having a simple plan — record, protect stock, check the basics, then call — makes it far easier to handle.</p>', '/images/commercial-display-freezer-1200.webp', 'Commercial refrigerated display case stocked with chilled food', 'Commercial Refrigeration', 'Cold Room, Southall, Hounslow, Hayes, Heathrow', 'HS Appliances', 'Commercial Fridge Running Warm? A Food Business Checklist', 'Display fridge, dairy cabinet or cold room creeping above temperature? What to do first, the checks staff can safely make, and when to call an engineer.', 'published', '2026-11-08 09:00:00', '2026-11-08 09:00:00');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, featured_image, image_alt, category, tags, author, meta_title, meta_description, status, published_at, created_at) VALUES ('fridge-not-cooling-freezer-working', 'Fridge Not Cooling but the Freezer Still Works? What to Check First', 'Warm fridge, frozen freezer? Why it happens in frost-free fridge freezers, the checks you can safely do at home, and when it needs an engineer.', '<p>The milk is warm, the salad is wilting — but the ice cream in the freezer is still rock solid. If that sounds familiar, you&#39;re not alone. A fridge that&#39;s stopped cooling while the freezer carries on working is one of the most common fridge freezer faults, and it has a very specific set of causes.</p>
<p>Here&#39;s why it happens, what you can check yourself, and when it&#39;s time to call in an engineer.</p>
<h2>Why the freezer works but the fridge doesn&#39;t</h2>
<p>Most modern fridge freezers are <strong>frost-free</strong>. They don&#39;t have a separate cooling system for the fridge. Instead, there&#39;s a single cold coil (the evaporator) in the freezer, and a small fan blows cold air from the freezer into the fridge through a duct, controlled by a flap called a <strong>damper</strong>.</p>
<p>So if anything stops that cold air reaching the fridge, the freezer keeps freezing while the fridge slowly warms up. The usual suspects are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ice blocking the evaporator or the air duct</strong> — usually because the automatic defrost system has a fault.</li>
<li><strong>A failed evaporator fan</strong> — no fan, no airflow.</li>
<li><strong>A stuck or faulty damper</strong> — the flap stays shut.</li>
<li><strong>A faulty fridge temperature sensor or control board</strong> — the fridge never asks for cold air.</li>
</ul>
<p>On older, non-frost-free models with a separate fridge cooling plate, the causes are different — often a failed thermostat or a problem with the plate itself.</p>
<h2>First: protect your food</h2>
<p>Keep the doors closed as much as possible. A fridge thermometer is the easiest way to know where you stand: food safety guidance is to keep a fridge between <strong>0°C and 5°C</strong>.</p>
<p>If high-risk food — meat, fish, dairy, cooked rice and leftovers — has been warm for several hours, the safe choice is to throw it away. When in doubt, throw it out. Move what you can to a cool box or a neighbour&#39;s fridge while you sort the problem out.</p>
<h2>Checks you can do yourself</h2>
<h3>1. The temperature setting</h3>
<p>Check the fridge setting hasn&#39;t been knocked or changed — it happens more often than you&#39;d think, especially with touch controls. Some models also have a &quot;holiday&quot; or &quot;vacation&quot; mode that lets the fridge warm up deliberately.</p>
<h3>2. Blocked vents inside the fridge</h3>
<p>Look for the air outlet, usually at the top or back of the fridge compartment. If it&#39;s blocked by a tall bottle, a packed shelf or a bag of salad pressed against it, cold air can&#39;t get in. Clear the space around it and give it a few hours.</p>
<h3>3. Door seals</h3>
<p>Warm, humid air leaking in through a split or loose seal causes more frost in the freezer and makes the fridge work harder. Try the paper test: close the door on a sheet of paper. If it slides out with no resistance, the seal isn&#39;t doing its job.</p>
<h3>4. Space to breathe</h3>
<p>Fridge freezers get rid of heat through the back or underneath. If yours is squeezed into a tight alcove, pushed hard against the wall or surrounded by clutter, it can struggle — particularly in summer. Make sure there&#39;s a gap behind and above, and vacuum any dust off the coils at the back.</p>
<h3>5. Listen for the fan</h3>
<p>Open the freezer and listen. On many models you&#39;ll hear the fan running when the appliance is cooling (some pause the fan when the door is open — try pressing the door switch with your finger). A <strong>grinding or ticking</strong> noise often means ice is catching the fan blades. <strong>Silence</strong> can mean the fan has failed.</p>
<h3>6. The manual defrost test</h3>
<p>If you suspect ice is the problem, a manual defrost is a useful test:</p>
<ol>
<li>Move your food somewhere cold.</li>
<li>Switch the fridge freezer off at the wall and leave both doors open for <strong>12 to 24 hours</strong>, with towels on the floor to catch water.</li>
<li>Switch it back on and give it several hours to come down to temperature.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the fridge cools properly again, an ice blockage was the cause. But if the problem comes back within days or weeks, the <strong>automatic defrost system has a fault</strong> — usually the defrost heater, a defrost sensor or the timer/control board — and the ice will keep returning until it&#39;s repaired.</p>
<h2>When to call an engineer</h2>
<p>It&#39;s worth booking a repair if:</p>
<ul>
<li>The fridge stays warm even after a manual defrost.</li>
<li>It warms up again within days or weeks of defrosting (a defrost fault).</li>
<li>The evaporator fan is silent, or you can hear it grinding.</li>
<li>You see an error code or a flashing temperature display.</li>
<li>The compressor (the black unit at the back, near the bottom) is clicking on and off repeatedly or isn&#39;t running at all.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fans, defrost heaters, sensors and dampers are usually well worth replacing. Faults in the sealed refrigeration system — such as a refrigerant leak or a failed compressor — are a bigger job, and on an older or cheaper appliance a replacement may make more sense. A good engineer will tell you which side of that line you&#39;re on before any work is done.</p>
<p>If you&#39;re in Twickenham, Richmond, Isleworth, Ealing or elsewhere in West London, our <a href="/services/fridge-freezer-repair/">fridge freezer repair service</a> covers all of these faults, including <a href="/services/american-fridge-repair/">American-style fridge freezers</a> with ice makers and water dispensers. Check whether we <a href="/areas/">cover your area</a>, or <a href="/book/?service=fridge-freezer-repair">book a repair online</a>.</p>
<h2>Keeping your fridge freezer healthy</h2>
<ul>
<li>Leave a gap around the appliance for airflow, and vacuum the coils once or twice a year.</li>
<li>Let leftovers cool before putting them in.</li>
<li>Don&#39;t overfill shelves or block the air vents.</li>
<li>Check door seals now and then, and wipe them clean — sticky seals tear more easily.</li>
<li>Keep a fridge thermometer inside so you notice problems early.</li>
</ul>', '/images/fridge-door-open-shelves-1200.webp', 'Fridge freezer with the fridge door open and a few drinks inside', 'Fridges & Freezers', 'Fridge Freezer, Twickenham, Richmond, Isleworth, Ealing', 'HS Appliances', 'Fridge Not Cooling but Freezer Works? What to Check', 'Warm fridge, frozen freezer? Why it happens in frost-free fridge freezers, the checks you can safely do at home, and when it needs an engineer.', 'published', '2026-11-08 09:00:00', '2026-11-08 09:00:00');
INSERT INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, featured_image, image_alt, category, tags, author, meta_title, meta_description, status, published_at, created_at) VALUES ('washing-machine-not-draining', 'Why Is My Washing Machine Not Draining? A Feltham Engineer''s Guide', 'Water left in the drum? The most common reasons a washing machine won''t drain, the safe checks you can do yourself, and when to call an engineer.', '<p>You open the door at the end of a cycle and there&#39;s still water in the drum — or the machine has stopped mid-wash with an error code flashing. A washing machine that won&#39;t drain is one of the most common faults we&#39;re asked about in Feltham and across West London, and the good news is that the cause is often simple.</p>
<p>This guide walks through the checks you can safely do yourself, in the order we&#39;d do them, and explains the point at which it&#39;s worth calling an engineer.</p>
<h2>First: make it safe</h2>
<p>Before you touch anything:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Switch the machine off at the wall and unplug it.</strong> Water and electricity don&#39;t mix.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#39;t force the door.</strong> Most machines keep the door locked while there&#39;s water inside. Forcing it can break the door interlock — and flood your floor.</li>
<li><strong>Get towels and a shallow tray ready.</strong> A full drum can hold more water than you&#39;d expect.</li>
</ul>
<h2>1. Check it&#39;s not just the programme</h2>
<p>It sounds obvious, but some programmes deliberately pause with water in the drum — for example a &quot;rinse hold&quot; or &quot;anti-crease&quot; option, or some wool and delicate cycles. Check the display and your manual. If the machine is simply waiting for you, selecting a drain or spin programme will empty it.</p>
<h2>2. Look at the drain hose</h2>
<p>Pull the machine out carefully and look at the grey drain hose at the back:</p>
<ul>
<li>Is it <strong>kinked or squashed</strong> behind the machine? This happens easily when a machine is pushed back into a tight space.</li>
<li>Is it pushed <strong>too far down the standpipe</strong>? If the end of the hose sits below the water level in the pipe, it can siphon or back up.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3. Check the sink waste or standpipe</h2>
<p>If your machine drains into the sink trap under the kitchen sink, that connection can block with grease and food debris from the sink — especially if the sink itself is draining slowly.</p>
<p>A classic problem after a <strong>new sink, tap or trap has been fitted</strong>: the plumber&#39;s blanking cap on the washing machine spigot of the new trap was never removed. If your machine stopped draining right after kitchen work, check that first.</p>
<h2>4. Clean the pump filter</h2>
<p>This is the single most common fix. Most front-loading machines have a filter behind a small flap at the bottom front corner. It catches coins, hair grips, buttons, bra wires and fluff before they reach the pump.</p>
<p>To clean it:</p>
<ol>
<li>Make sure the machine is <strong>off and unplugged</strong>.</li>
<li>Lay towels down and put a shallow tray or baking dish under the flap.</li>
<li>If there&#39;s a small emergency drain tube next to the filter, pull it out and drain the water into the tray first — it&#39;s slower but much less messy.</li>
<li><strong>Unscrew the filter slowly, anticlockwise.</strong> Water will come out — stop and empty the tray as needed.</li>
<li>Remove debris from the filter and the opening behind it, then rinse the filter.</li>
<li>Screw it back in firmly and straight. A cross-threaded filter will leak.</li>
</ol>
<p>Run a drain or spin programme to test. If the machine now drains, the blockage was the problem.</p>
<h2>5. Listen to the pump</h2>
<p>Run a drain programme and listen near the bottom of the machine:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A normal whirring with water flowing out</strong> — the pump is fine; any blockage is further down the line.</li>
<li><strong>A hum but no water moving</strong> — the pump may be jammed by something lodged in the impeller, or the pump motor may have failed.</li>
<li><strong>Silence</strong> — the pump isn&#39;t getting power, which can mean a failed pump, a wiring fault or a control board problem.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. What the error code is telling you</h2>
<p>Many machines show a specific code when they can&#39;t drain. Common examples include <strong>OE</strong> on LG machines, <strong>5E / 5C</strong> on Samsung, <strong>E18 / F18</strong> on Bosch and <strong>F05</strong> on Hotpoint and Indesit — but codes vary between models, so check your manual. A drain code confirms the machine knows it can&#39;t empty; it doesn&#39;t tell you whether the cause is a blockage, the pump or the electronics.</p>
<h2>When to call an engineer</h2>
<p>It&#39;s time to get it looked at if:</p>
<ul>
<li>The filter, hose and sink connection are all clear but the machine still won&#39;t drain.</li>
<li>The pump hums but doesn&#39;t move water, or makes no sound at all.</li>
<li>The door won&#39;t release even after the drum has been emptied.</li>
<li>The machine is tripping the electrics or you can see water leaking from underneath.</li>
</ul>
<p>At that point the likely culprits are the drain pump, the sump hose between the drum and the pump, the pressure switch that tells the machine how much water is in the drum, or the main control board. An engineer can test each one rather than guessing — which matters, because they&#39;re very different repairs.</p>
<p>If you&#39;re in Feltham, Hounslow, Ashford, Hayes or nearby, our <a href="/services/washing-machine-repair/">washing machine repair service</a> covers exactly these faults. We diagnose the problem, explain it in plain English and give you a price before any work starts. See <a href="/areas/">all the areas we cover</a>, or <a href="/book/?service=washing-machine-repair">book a repair online</a>.</p>
<h2>How to stop it happening again</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check pockets</strong> before every wash. Coins and hair grips cause more pump problems than anything else.</li>
<li><strong>Use a wash bag</strong> for underwired bras — loose wires are a frequent find in pump filters.</li>
<li><strong>Don&#39;t overdose detergent.</strong> Too much foam can trigger drain and foam errors, especially with high-efficiency machines.</li>
<li><strong>Clean the pump filter every few months</strong>, not just when there&#39;s a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Run a hot maintenance wash</strong> with a machine cleaner from time to time to clear grease and residue from the sump and hoses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Still stuck? <a href="/contact/">Call us or send a message</a> with the make, model and any error code, and we&#39;ll talk you through the next step.</p>', '/images/washing-machine-drum-close-up-1200.webp', 'Inside view of an empty washing machine drum', 'Laundry', 'Washing Machine, Feltham, Hounslow, Ashford, Hayes', 'HS Appliances', 'Washing Machine Not Draining? Feltham Engineer''s Guide', 'Water left in the drum? The most common reasons a washing machine won''t drain, the safe checks you can do yourself, and when to call an engineer.', 'published', '2026-11-08 09:00:00', '2026-11-08 09:00:00');

